    return -1;
}
out_str[0] = 0;
flb_unescape_string_utf8((const char *)value, value_len, out_str);
out_len = strlen(out_str);
```

The `flb_unescape_string_utf8` function has a boundary check `esc_out > sz-count_out` where `sz = value_len`. So it won't write more than `value_len` bytes. But wait - let me re-read the boundary check more carefully:

```c
esc_out = u8_wc_toutf8(temp, ch);
if (esc_out > sz-count_out) {
    flb_error("Crossing over string boundary");
    break;
}
```

`sz` is `value_len` (the input size). `count_out` is the number of output bytes written so far. So the check ensures `count_out + esc_out <= sz`. This means the output can't exceed `sz` bytes. Since `out_str` is `value_len + 1` bytes, this is safe.

Hmm, but wait. Let me look at `u8_read_escape_sequence` more carefully. When we have `\u` escape, it reads up to 4 hex digits. But what if the escape sequence is `\u` followed by fewer than 4 hex digits? Let me trace through:

```c
else if (str[0] == 'u') {
